              {
                "type": "reasoning.text",
                "text": "Okay, let me unpack this AITA question. The user is a college student in Britain (American high school background) who just got home to find out their great aunt Wyn died. They've never heard of this person before, met her only once as a very young child (1-2 years old), and have no memory of her. Their mom is guilt-tripping them into attending the funeral \" out of respect\" and \" support,\" but the user feels like they'd be gatecrashing since they don't know the deceased or most of the extended family. \n\nFirst, I need to clarify the core ethical tension here: family obligation versus personal boundaries. The user's mom frames it as \" respect for the family,\" but the user's perspective is that they're a stranger to the deceased. \n\nHmm... let's break down the facts:\n- \"Great aunt\" means the user's grandmother's sister. \n- User met her only once as a toddler (1-2 years old), so no meaningful relationship exists. \n- Mom never mentioned this person before, which suggests she wasn't close to her either (or at least didn't consider her relevant to the user's life). \n- The user identifies as \"awkward at funerals for people they've met more than once,\" so this would be even more uncomfortable. \n- Mom's \" support\" argument feels like emotional manipulation (\" I'll go on my own\" in a sulky way). \n\nNow, what does \" respect\" mean here? In many cultures, funerals are seen as family obligations, but respect isn't about being physically present if you have no connection to the deceased. Respect for the *deceased* would involve knowing them; respect for the * living* family might involve offering condolences, but not necessarily attending a funeral where you're a stranger. \n\nI recall that in Jewish tradition (which the user mentioned earlier in the conversation), there's a concept of \" * kavod hamet\"* (respect for the dead), but that's about how * we* treat the body and funeral, not about forced attendance by distant relatives. 